). \n\nFirst, we note that \\( x^2 \\) must be a value that allows the sum to be 10. Since \\( x^2 \\) is non-negative, we have:\n\\[ x^2 \\leq 10. \\]\nThis implies:\n\\[ -\\sqrt{10} \\leq x \\leq \\sqrt{10}. \\]\nApproximating, we get:\n\\[ -3.162 \\leq x \\leq 3.162. \\]\n\nNext, we consider the integer parts \\( \\left\\lfloor \\frac{x}{2} \\right\\rfloor \\) and \\( \\left\\lfloor \\frac{x}{3} \\right\\rfloor \\). These values depend on the intervals of \\( x \\). We will test different intervals of \\( x \\) to find the solutions.\n\n### Case 1: \\( x \\in [3, 4) \\)\nIn this interval, we have:\n\\[ \\left\\lfloor \\frac{x}{2} \\right\\rfloor = 1 \\]\n\\[ \\left\\lfloor \\frac{x}{3} \\right\\rfloor = 1 \\]\nThus, the equation becomes:\n\\[ x^2 + 1 + 1 = 10 \\]\n\\[ x^2 + 2 = 10 \\]\n\\[ x^2 = 8 \\]\n\\[ x = \\pm \\sqrt{8} = \\pm 2\\sqrt{2} \\approx \\pm 2.828 \\]\nSince \\( x \\in [3, 4) \\), \\( x = \\sqrt{8} \\) is a valid solution.\n\n### Case 2: \\( x \\in [2, 3) \\)\nIn this interval, we have:\n\\[ \\left\\lfloor \\frac{x}{2} \\right\\rfloor = 1 \\]\n\\[ \\left\\lfloor \\frac{x}{3} \\right\\rfloor = 0 \\]\nThus, the equation becomes:\n\\[ x^2 + 1 + 0 = 10 \\]\n\\[ x^2 + 1 = 10 \\]\n\\[ x^2 = 9 \\]\n\\[ x = \\pm 3 \\]\nSince \\( x \\in [2, 3) \\), \\( x = 3 \\) is not a valid solution.\n\n### Case 3: \\( x \\in [1, 2) \\)\nIn this interval, we have:\n\\[ \\left\\lfloor \\frac{x}{2} \\right\\rfloor = 0 \\]\n\\[ \\left\\lfloor \\frac{x}{3} \\right\\rfloor = 0 \\]\nThus, the equation becomes:\n\\[ x^2 + 0 + 0 = 10 \\]\n\\[ x^2 = 10 \\]\n\\[ x = \\pm \\sqrt{10} \\]\nSince \\( x \\in [1, 2) \\), \\( x = \\sqrt{10} \\) is not a valid solution.\n\n### Case 4: \\( x \\in [0, 1) \\)\nIn this interval, we have:\n\\[ \\left\\lfloor \\frac{x}{2} \\right\\rfloor = 0 \\]\n\\[ \\left\\lfloor \\frac{x}{3} \\right\\rfloor = 0 \\]\nThus, the equation becomes:\n\\[ x^2 + 0 + 0 = 10 \\]\n\\[ x^2 = 10 \\]\n\\[ x = \\pm \\sqrt{10} \\]\nSince \\( x \\in [0, 1) \\), \\( x = \\sqrt{10} \\) is not a valid solution.\n\n### Case 5: \\( x \\in [-1, 0) \\)\nIn this interval, we have:\n\\[ \\left\\lfloor \\frac{x}{2} \\right\\rfloor = -1 \\]\n\\[ \\left\\lfloor \\frac{x}{3} \\right\\rfloor = -1 \\]\nThus, the equation becomes:\n\\[ x^2 - 1 - 1 = 10 \\]\n\\[ x^2 - 2 = 10 \\]\n\\[ x^2 = 12 \\]\n\\[ x = \\pm \\sqrt{12} = \\pm 2\\sqrt{3} \\]\nSince \\( x \\in [-1, 0) \\), \\( x = -\\sqrt{12} \\) is not a valid solution.\n\n### Case 6: \\( x \\in [-2, -1) \\)\nIn this interval, we have:\n\\[ \\left\\lfloor \\frac{x}{2} \\right\\rfloor = -1 \\]\n\\[ \\left\\lfloor \\frac{x}{3} \\right\\rfloor = -1 \\]\nThus, the equation becomes:\n\\[ x^2 - 1 - 1 = 10 \\]\n\\[ x^2 - 2 = 10 \\]\n\\[ x^2 = 12 \\]\n\\[ x = \\pm \\sqrt{12} = \\pm 2\\sqrt{3} \\]\nSince \\( x \\in [-2, -1) \\), \\( x = -\\sqrt{12} \\) is not a valid solution.\n\n### Case 7: \\( x \\in [-3, -2) \\)\nIn this interval, we have:\n\\[ \\left\\lfloor \\frac{x}{2} \\right\\rfloor = -2 \\]\n\\[ \\left\\lfloor \\frac{x}{3} \\right\\rfloor = -1 \\]\nThus, the equation becomes:\n\\[ x^2 - 2 - 1 = 10 \\]\n\\[ x^2 - 3 = 10 \\]\n\\[ x^2 = 13 \\]\n\\[ x = \\pm \\sqrt{13} \\]\nSince \\( x \\in [-3, -2) \\), \\( x = -\\sqrt{13} \\) is not a valid solution.\n\n### Case 8: \\( x \\in (-\\infty, -3) \\)\nIn this interval, we have:\n\\[ \\left\\lfloor \\frac{x}{2} \\right\\rfloor \\leq -2 \\]\n\\[ \\left\\lfloor \\frac{x}{3} \\right\\rfloor \\leq -2 \\]\nThus, the equation becomes:\n\\[ x^2 + \\left\\lfloor \\frac{x}{2} \\right\\rfloor + \\left\\lfloor \\frac{x}{3} \\right\\rfloor \\leq x^2 - 4 \\]\nSince \\( x^2 \\leq 10 \\), \\( x^2 - 4 \\leq 6 \\), which is not enough to reach 10.\n\nTherefore, the only valid solution is \\( x = 2\\sqrt{2} \\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2\\sqrt{2}}\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{2\\sqrt{2}}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true}}
